# The accounts a demo offers on its sign-in page.
#
# Directory data, not a list in the code, because that is what they are: a visitor signs in
# by binding to the directory as one of them, so the directory is the thing that decides
# whether an account exists. A Java constant saying otherwise is a second source of truth
# that goes stale the moment somebody edits one of them - and a sign-in page printing a
# username the directory has never heard of is worse than printing nothing.
#
# Loaded into the demo's own generated directory at startup. The suffix is substituted, so
# this file says ou=people and the demo puts it under whatever base it was configured with.
#
# Three attributes are this application's own, and carry what the sign-in page prints:
#
#   demoRole      what to call the account, and the order it is listed in
#   demoSummary   one line on what this role gets that the others do not
#   demoOrder     where it sits in the list, most to least
#
# They exist only here. A demo pointed at a real directory finds no entry carrying them and
# offers no accounts, which is the right answer: those people are not in it.
#
# Every one of them carries the password the demo is configured with, substituted in below.
# It is not a credential - these people are invented and the directory holding them goes
# with the process.

dn: uid=demo.admin,ou=people,{base}
objectClass: top
objectClass: person
objectClass: organizationalPerson
objectClass: inetOrgPerson
objectClass: icOrgPerson
uid: demo.admin
userPassword: {password}
cn: Dana Okafor
sn: Okafor
givenName: Dana
displayName: Dana Okafor
icEmail: demo.admin@intelink.ic.gov
internetEmail: demo.admin@ugov.gov
title: Information Assurance Officer
employeeType: Civilian
countryOfAffiliation: USA
dutyOrganization: Example Agency
dutySubOrganization: Enterprise IT
isICMember: TRUE
demoRole: Administrator
demoOrder: 1
demoSummary: Everything, plus the administration page: the whole audit trail, the managed
  attributes, and the controls that run the jobs.

dn: uid=demo.project,ou=people,{base}
objectClass: top
objectClass: person
objectClass: organizationalPerson
objectClass: inetOrgPerson
objectClass: icOrgPerson
